-ae7f-783108cafb87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338698/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8c90a1-b232-524e-a1e4-4bcccc11b470" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338698/field-3/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7" :answer-field/choices ["q-338698/field-3/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" "q-338698/field-3/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338698/field-4/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" :answer/id #uuid "bba61dad-a8dc-5792-9577-b5a5f3fbef2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338698/field-4/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7" :answer/id #uuid "c303f994-a25c-5372-be86-56a56f70c86d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338698/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "821eacb2-cbf1-57d1-80d1-bf21e19ab59a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-338698/field-4/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7" :answer-field/choices ["q-338698/field-4/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" "q-338698/field-4/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338698" :question/id #uuid "c9739c57-3897-599c-b0c1-49ba979c7c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-338698" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(x+1){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\n$$\ny(-2)=0,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(x+1){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(-2)=0,\\,y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, and the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{homogeneous}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338698/field-1" "q-338698/field-2" "q-338698/field-3" "q-338698/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338698"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21795"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- $y'' = x\\cos(x), \\quad y(2)=7$\n\n- $y'' + 3y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n\n- $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21800"] :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny''(x) + 2y(x) = x,  \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n- Robin\n\n- Dirichlet\n\n- Neumann\n\n- Periodic" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\ny'(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a) = y(b), \\qquad y'(a)  = y'(b)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i,$ and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x = a = 0$ and $x = b = 2.$\n\n$$\ny(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21796"] :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0,  \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/c1e4a45a8051dccc92a71ca2cf0b3851cfb5c6c5c22e2fc416ebcc8aeba96740.png).\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/077041b590cf1030028330dae6748828deef81c3d8d6a1c70994a90407ac05a2.png) boundary conditions are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/50f935ce3a16bbb94697f74737b4bba66dc9ce01d6629e007ff64f46347c1ccb.png)\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/339c256afc594fe366f0fdcb008556ef43b33be529fed25c65e7faa077166be2.png)." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\ny'(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i,$ and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., $\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0,$ the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
